Configure firewalld
===============================================================
Goal: Make sure firewalld is running, set 'public' as the default
zone, allow HTTP and block ping on public, create a new zone
'office', add source 192.168.10.0/24 to it, and allow HTTP, HTTPS,
SSH and port 8443/tcp on office. All rules must be permanent.

Network used: 192.168.10.0/24 (internal office network)
Interface   : ens33 (check yours with: ip addr show)


STEP 1: Check firewall status
-----------------------------
$ sudo systemctl status firewalld

Expected: active (running). Press q to leave the status screen.

If it is not running:
$ sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld

Alternative quick check:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --state
(prints: running)


STEP 2: Set the default zone to 'public'
----------------------------------------
$ sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Verify:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --get-default-zone
(prints: public)


STEP 3: On the public zone: allow HTTP, block ping
--------------------------------------------------
See the current public zone settings first:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all

Allow HTTP permanently:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=http --permanent

Block ping (ICMP echo-request) permanently:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-icmp-block=echo-request --permanent

Apply the changes:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Verify:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all
(services should include http, icmp-blocks should show echo-request)

Test the ping block (from ANOTHER machine, not the server itself):
$ ping <server-ip>
(should get no reply)

NOTE: Your screenshot used --add-icmp-block-inversion. That flips
the ICMP rules (blocks everything except the listed types) and
does not directly "drop echo-request" as the task says.
Use --add-icmp-block=echo-request as shown above. If you already
ran the inversion command, undo it with:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --remove-icmp-block-inversion


STEP 4: Create a new zone 'office'
----------------------------------
$ sudo firewall-cmd --new-zone=office --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Verify the zone exists:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --get-zones
(the list should include: office)


STEP 5: Add source 192.168.10.0/24 to the 'office' zone
-------------------------------------------------------
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-source=192.168.10.0/24 --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload

(Type "office" carefully. A typo such as "offic" gives:
 Error: INVALID_ZONE)

Verify:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --list-sources


STEP 6: On 'office': allow HTTP, HTTPS, SSH and port 8443/tcp
-------------------------------------------------------------
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=http --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=https --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=ssh --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-port=8443/tcp --permanent
$ sudo firewall-cmd --reload

(SSH is in the task box of your doc but not in your screenshots,
 so I added it. The practical's list at the top of your doc says
 only HTTP, HTTPS and 8443/tcp. Keep the SSH line to match the
 task box, or skip it if your teacher wants only the three.)


STEP 7: Final verification (rules survive reboot)
-------------------------------------------------
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --list-all
$ sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all
$ sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=office --list-all

Expected for 'office':
  sources: 192.168.10.0/24
  services: http https ssh
  ports: 8443/tcp

Expected for 'public':
  services: (includes) http
  icmp-blocks: echo-request

Check the default zone is still public:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --get-default-zone


IMPORTANT: DO NOT run "firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=office"
--------------------------------------------------------------
The last line of your screenshot sets the default zone to office.
The task says the default zone must be 'public'. Setting it to
office would make the office rules apply to ALL traffic that is not
matched by a source. If you already ran it, fix it with:
$ sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public


QUICK COMMAND SUMMARY
---------------------
sudo systemctl status firewalld                     -> check status
sudo firewall-cmd --set-default-zone=public         -> default zone
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-service=http --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-icmp-block=echo-request --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --new-zone=office --permanent     -> new zone
sudo firewall-cmd --reload                          -> apply
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-source=192.168.10.0/24 --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=http --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=https --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-service=ssh --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --add-port=8443/tcp --permanent
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=office --list-all          -> verify
sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-all          -> verify
